page cmd_port {
  title = "vmlab port"
  chapter_header "vmlab port" {
    kicker       = "Commands · reference"
    reading_time = "3 min read"
    updated      = "2026-10-29"
    version      = "vmlab 0.35"
  }

  p <<DOC
    `vmlab port` prints the host port that a running machine's forward to a
    guest port is bound on, and nothing else. It is written for scripts: a
    test reaches a guest's web server through it without parsing
    [vmlab status](cmd_status) or knowing whether the host port was
    declared, picked automatically, or shifted by a
    [worktree lab's port slot](guide_worktrees).
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab port [OPTIONS] <MACHINE> <GUEST-PORT>" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Meaning" |
      | "`<MACHINE>`" | "The machine, as `[lab/]machine`. Without a lab, the lab comes from the working directory, with `--instance` or `VMLAB_INSTANCE` applied, as for other machine verbs." |
      | "`<GUEST-PORT>`" | "The guest port, as `<port>`, `<port>/tcp` or `<port>/udp`, with a port from 1 to 65535. The suffix is case-insensitive." |
      | "`--json`" | "Print the forward's status entry as JSON instead of the bare port." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  h2 "How a forward is matched"

  p <<DOC
    The verb reads the lab's status, the same value `vmlab status` reads, and
    looks at the forwards installed for the machine: segment `forward {}`
    blocks, container `port {}` blocks and runtime `Segment.forward` calls
    alike. A forward matches when its guest port is the one given and, when
    a suffix is given, it carries that protocol. A forward declared with
    `proto = "both"` matches `/tcp`, `/udp` and no suffix.
  DOC

  list {
    li "Several matches bound on one host port are one answer: the first, in the order `vmlab status` lists them."
    li "Matches bound on different host ports are ambiguous. When a `/tcp` or `/udp` suffix would settle it, the error asks for one. Otherwise it lists every candidate."
    li "A stopped machine has no answer, even while its forwards are still listed in the lab's status."
  }

  p <<DOC
    The verb is read-only. It never starts a lab daemon: a lab with none
    running has no forwards installed, so the answer is that the lab is not
    running. It works for every lab, frozen or not.
  DOC

  h2 "Output"

  p <<DOC
    On success the verb prints the host port and a newline. With `--json` it
    prints the forward's entry from the `forwards` list of
    `vmlab status --json`: `machine`, `segment`, `host_port`, `guest`
    (`ip:port`), `proto`, `auto`, `guest_port`, and `declared_port` when a
    worktree lab's port slot shifted a fixed port off its declared one.
  DOC

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
$ vmlab port web 80/tcp
18580
$ curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:$(vmlab port web 80/tcp)/"
$ vmlab port portcheck/web 443 --json
{
  "auto": true,
  "guest": "10.10.0.20:443",
  "guest_port": 443,
  "host_port": 33177,
  "machine": "web",
  "proto": "tcp",
  "segment": "lan"
}
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    In a worktree lab, a forward whose fixed port was shifted carries the
    port it declared:
  DOC

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
$ vmlab port web 80 --json
{
  "auto": false,
  "declared_port": 18080,
  "guest": "10.10.0.20:80",
  "guest_port": 80,
  "host_port": 18081,
  "machine": "web",
  "proto": "tcp",
  "segment": "lan"
}
SRC
  }

  h2 "Errors"

  table {
    rows:
      | "Case" | "Exit" | "Message" |
      | "The guest port or protocol is malformed." | "2 (`invalid_argument`)" | "Names the argument, then says the protocol is `tcp` or `udp`, or that a guest port runs from 1 to 65535." |
      | "A tcp and a udp forward are bound on different host ports, and no suffix was given." | "2 (`invalid_argument`)" | "`\"web\" has more than one forward to guest port 80: 80/tcp on host port 18580, 80/udp on host port 58525; name one as 80/tcp or 80/udp`" |
      | "No installed forward matches." | "4 (`not_found`)" | "`\"web\" in lab \"portcheck\" has no forward to guest port 81; its forwards: 80/tcp on host port 18580, …`, or `…; it has no forwards installed`" |
      | "The lab declares no such machine." | "4 (`not_found`)" | "`no vm or container \"nope\" in lab \"portcheck\"`" |
      | "The machine is stopped." | "5 (`conflict`)" | "`\"web\" in lab \"portcheck\" is not running`" |
      | "The lab has no daemon running." | "5 (`conflict`)" | "`lab \"portcheck-b\" is not running`" |
  }

  p <<DOC
    Exit status is 0 on success. A directory with no lab file, given no
    `lab/machine` reference, fails as other verbs do, with exit 1.
  DOC

  h2 "See also"

  list {
    li "[vmlab status](cmd_status): every installed forward, with automatic and shifted ports marked."
    li "[Networking](guide_networking): how forwards are planned and bound."
    li "[Worktree labs](guide_worktrees): port slots and the shift."
  }
}
